+- Use it proactively when a task has 3 or more distinct steps, or when the user gives several tasks or asks for a todo list.
+- Skip it for a single simple task or a purely conversational request. When in doubt, use it.
 
-- Send the full list on every call; it replaces the previous one.
+How it works:
+- Send the full list on every call; it replaces the previous one. There is no partial update — items you leave out are removed.
+- Each item pairs a brief `content` description with a `status`: pending, in_progress, completed, or cancelled.
 - Mark items `in_progress` when you start them and `completed` as soon as they're actually done.
-- Only call it when the list changes.
+- Only call it when the list changes. The result echoes the full list with per-status counts.
+
+Batching: sequential — issue at most one todowrite call per block. Every call replaces the entire list, so two calls in one block race: whichever finishes last wins and the other update is lost. Make the next todowrite call in a later step, once the work it records has happened.
